    "content": "\"Read option immersion-coolants \\\"Generative search for single-phase immersion coolants\\\" in the option record below. I'm thinking about running something on this with up to $1,000 of GPU credit at Lambda's on-demand prices (https://lambda.ai/pricing). Make it concrete: what to build, the baseline, how to check it's right, and the cost. Cite a URL for every factual claim. Write to /home/hal/dev/interp/latent_open/experiments/immersion-coolants/20261018-0037-loop/out/r0/card/work/seed-1/seed/idea/63a459fc/producer.md. Option record: {\\\"id\\\":\\\"immersion-coolants\\\",\\\"title\\\":\\\"Generative search for single-phase immersion coolants\\\",\\\"summary\\\":\\\"Data-centre immersion cooling needs dielectric liquids with low viscosity, high thermal conductivity and heat capacity, a low dielectric constant and a high flash point. PFAS rules and the end of Novec production are pushing the field towards PFAS-free hydrocarbons and esters. Study whether a generative search over molecules and mixtures finds candidates that beat known commercial single-phase fluids on a cooling figure of merit. More compute buys more candidates checked by GPU molecular dynamics after predictor filtering, and longer simulations per candidate. Score against measured viscosity, thermal conductivity and dielectric data for known fluids held out of training, never against the predictors themselves.\\\",\\\"root_link\\\":\\\"SAME: a concrete version of the asker's line, kept on discovery and on transport properties (viscosity, thermal conductivity).\\\",\\\"compute_open\\\":\\\"Candidates checked by GPU molecular dynamics (about 10^2 to 10^4) and simulation length per candidate. Unknown: whether the hit rate against known commercial fluids keeps rising with more candidates, or whether predictor and force-field error caps it.\\\",\\\"sequential\\\":\\\"not given\\\",\\\"source_urls\\\":[\\\"https://espace2.etsmtl.ca/id/eprint/31955/\\\",\\\"https://blog.se.com/datacenter/2026/05/22/pfas-phase-out-liquid-cooling-us-data-center-operators-must-do/\\\",\\\"https://opencompute.org/documents/ocp-acs-immersion-requirements-rev-2-v1-00-pdf\\\"]}. Root question: generative pipeline for discovery of single phase immersion coolants. Read only pages you fetch and what you write under /home/hal/dev/interp/latent_open/experiments/immersion-coolants/20261018-0037-loop/out/r0/card/work/seed-1/seed/idea/63a459fc; write only under /home/hal/dev/interp/latent_open/experiments/immersion-coolants/20261018-0037-loop/out/r0/card/work/seed-1/seed/idea/63a459fc. When the plan is written, return it as the record in your output schema; producer.md stays your working file. In the record, put every number the cost or the size of the test depends on in numbers, each with a basis: CITED (a page states this number for this same setting; give the page text that shows it), PROXY (a page states it for a different model, GPU, dataset, size or component; give the page text and say what differs), MEASURED (you ran code: save the script and its output under /home/hal/dev/interp/latent_open/experiments/immersion-coolants/20261018-0037-loop/out/r0/card/work/seed-1/seed/idea/63a459fc/pilot/ and give the output line and the machine), DERIVED (an expression over other ids), DESIGN (your choice), or GUESS (no source; say what it rests on). Give each rate as the per-GPU price and the GPU count, and each line's hours as an expression. The default line is a time box: DESIGN hours at a rate from the prices below, ended by a timer or stop rule; it needs no estimate of the work inside it, but if you have one, put it in need. Use GUESS only where a number drives the plan. For each PROXY or GUESS the cost or result depends on, name the phase that measures it in measured_by, and give that phase's threshold as gate if your plan changes on it. Optional: scenarios (pessimistic or sensitivity cases, as values for the inputs they change), optional lines for stretch work, instead_of for fallbacks, and yields (the number ids that say what the money buys: cases, samples, primes). Refer to the budget as budget_usd and to the price sheet's filesystem price as fs_usd_gib_month. List human time and money outside Lambda in outside_budget. predictions.if_lever and predictions.if_not are the outcomes you expect, on the same measured quantity, if more compute does or does not change the answer. predictions.noise_rule says how you will tell a real difference from run-to-run noise. predictions.expected is the outcome you expect, if_lever, if_not or unsure, with one sentence why. scored_against says what the measured outcome is compared with to decide it is correct. Each source carries the exact sentence from the page that you rely on. In restructuring, sequential_part is the part of this computation that one answer waits on step after step, and how many steps; method is why those steps need not run in order and how your plan computes them at the same time instead, or \\\"none\\\" if the plan only runs independent work side by side or splits each step across GPUs. If method is not none, also give condition, what must be true of this system for the method to pay off and the cheap measurement that checks it; condition_phase, the id of the phase that makes that measurement before the main spend; baseline_sequential, the fastest sequential implementation you will time and how it is tuned; and baseline_copies, independent copies run side by side on the same GPUs, which you will also time. Then compute_axis is the length of the chain or the number of runs the answer needs; predictions.if_lever and predictions.if_not say where on that axis, as numbers, the parallel version does or does not reach the same answer sooner than each baseline, and if_not says how you expect it to fail if it does; anchor checks the parallel result against the sequential one within a stated tolerance; test_summary names both baselines; and novelty.closest_prior is the nearest published parallel version of this computation. Write the record's card fields (question, hypothesis, predictions.if_lever, predictions.if_not, test_summary, novelty.closest_prior, novelty.left_open, anchor.what, limits, restructuring.sequential_part, restructuring.method) for a reader who has not seen your files: plain words, at most 25 words each (hypothesis and predictions.if_lever 22, anchor.what 24, novelty.closest_prior, novelty.left_open and limits 30, test_summary 45, restructuring.sequential_part 18, restructuring.method 22), no labels from your files or from the branch and framer files (write what C2 or E2 stands for, not the label), and keep every number the decision depends on. You may cite a source in any card field as [source id]. novelty.closest_prior is the published result that comes nearest to deciding between if_lever and if_not: what it measured and the largest amount of the compute quantity it used. novelty.prior_max_level is that amount in compute_axis.unit, or null if it is not on the same axis. novelty.decides says whether closest_prior already decides the outcome at your compute levels: no, partly or yes. novelty.searched says whether web search returned results while you planned. Run every local command under timeout. Start no new local run 45 minutes after you begin; if a run you need is unfinished by then, give its inputs basis GUESS with measured_by naming the phase that measures it. Local runs share a 4-core machine with other producers. Lambda's prices as read at the start of this run: {\\\"read_at\\\":\\\"2026-10-18\\\",\\\"instances\\\":[{\\\"gpu\\\":\\\"NVIDIA B200 SXM6\\\",\\\"gpu_memory_gb\\\":180,\\\"n_gpus\\\":8,\\\"per_gpu_usd_h\\\":6.69,\\\"page_text\\\":\\\"8x GPU tab: NVIDIA B200 SXM6 | VRAM/GPU: 180 GB | vCPUs: 208 | RAM: 2900 GiB | STORAGE: 22 TiB SSD | PRICE/GPU/HR*: $6.69 (* plus applicable sales tax/VAT/GST)\\\"},{\\\"gpu\\\":\\\"NVIDIA H100 SXM\\\",\\\"gpu_memory_gb\\\":80,\\\"n_gpus\\\":8,\\\"per_gpu_usd_h\\\":3.99,\\\"page_text\\\":\\\"8x