 should use the A6000≈A40 number 277 ns/day. But it's safer to use A100 267.5 (more conservative) or A40 277. Let me use ~270 as a PROXY from the A100/A40 numbers.\n\n3. **candidate_gpu_hours** (DERIVED): = candidate_md_ns / (md_throughput_ns_day / 24). With 20 ns at 270 ns/day = 20/11.25 = 1.78 GPU-hours. Round up for equilibration/overhead → I'll use a per-candidate GPU-hours of ~2.5 (add equilibration and setup). Let me define overhead factor.\n\nActually let me keep the derivation clean:\n- sim_len_ns = 20 (DESIGN, per candidate total production)\n- throughput_ns_day = 270 (PROXY)\n- ns_per_gpu_hour = throughput_ns_day / 24 = 11.25 (DERIVED)\n- md_gpu_hours_per_candidate = ceil(sim_len_ns / ns_per_gpu_hour) + overhead. Let me define md_hours_raw = sim_len_ns / ns_per_gpu_hour = 1.778, and add setup/equilibration overhead 25% → 2.22 → say 2.3.\n\nLet me define overhead_factor = 1.3 (DESIGN) to cover equilibration + setup + reruns. md_gpu_hours_per_candidate = 1.778 × 1.3 = 2.31 GPU-hours.\n\n4. **n_candidates_md**: This is the compute_axis. Levels: 100, 1000, 10000. The budget $1000 on A6000 at $1.09/hr = 917 GPU-hours. At 2.31 GPU-h/candidate → ~397 candidates. Hmm, that's only ~400 candidates for $1000 on A6000.\n\nWait, let me reconsider. With $1000:\n- A6000 $1.09/hr → 917 GPU-hours → ~397 candidates at 2.31 hr each. That's between 10^2 and 10^3.\n- If I use fewer ns per candidate (e.g., 5 ns total), 5/11.25 = 0.44 hr × 1.3 = 0.58 hr → ~1580 candidates.\n\nHmm. The option's compute axis is 10^2 to 10^4 candidates. $1000 gets me ~10^2.5 to 10^3 candidates at realistic MD cost. That's actually a fine and honest result: the full 10^4 scale is beyond $1000, and I should note that in full_scale (runs the budget can't buy).\n\nActually, let me reconsider the MD cost. For a screening study, you don't need full Green-Kubo convergence for every candidate — you need a ranking. A cheap but reliable approach: compute viscosity via Einstein relation from mean-squared displacement (needs ~1-5 ns), and thermal conductivity via Green-Kubo heat flux (needs ~5-10 ns). Let me use a shorter, cheaper per-candidate protocol to get more candidates:\n\nPer candidate: 3 replicas × 3 ns production = 9 ns, plus 1 ns equilibration = ~10 ns total → at 11.25 ns/hr = 0.89 hr, ×1.3 overhead = 1.16 GPU-hours.\n\nWith $1000 on A6000: 917 GPU-h / 1.16 = ~790 candidates. Still ~10^2.9.\n\nAlternatively use the cheapest viable GPU. A6000 at $1.09 is Lambda's cheapest modern GPU (besides Quadro RTX 6000 24GB at $0.69, which is old Turing, much slower MD). So A6000 is the sweet spot.\n\nActually wait — there's also the question of whether the whole $1000 should go to MD, or some to the predictor training (which is cheap, CPU/one GPU). Predictor training is negligible cost (a few GPU-hours on one A6000).\n\nLet me set the plan:\n\n**Plan**:\n1. Phase 1 (pilot/anchor): Reproduce MD transport properties of a known fluid (e.g., n-hexadecane or a small ester or PAO-2) with GROMACS/OpenMM, compare viscosity & thermal conductivity to experiment. This anchors the MD pipeline and calibrates throughput (MEASURED would be ideal, but no shell; I'll mark the throughput as PROXY and the anchor reproduction as a check). Budget ~10 GPU-hours.\n\n2. Phase 2 (predictor): Train/fine-tune a property predictor (following Org-Mol approach) on public property data (viscosity, thermal conductivity, dielectric constant) for hydrocarbons/esters, holding out known commercial fluids. This gives the filter. Cheap (~10-20 GPU-hours).\n\n3. Phase 3 (generation + filter): Generate ~10^4-10^5 candidate PFAS-free hydrocarbons/esters (SMILES enumeration), filter by dielectric constant <3.20, viscosity <12 cSt, thermal conductivity >0.140 W/m·K, flash point ≥150°C (from OCP), to get the top ~10^3 candidates for MD.\n\n4. Phase 4 (MD verification at 3 compute levels): Verify N candidates by MD (compute_axis levels 100 / 1000 / 10000), computing viscosity + thermal conductivity (and density, cp), then compute the cooling figure of merit (Mouromtseff number), compare to commercial baselines. Measure \"hit rate\" (fraction beating the best commercial FOM) and \"best FOM\" as a function of N.\n\n5. Score against held-out known commercial fluids' measured data (never the predictor). The anchor = MD reproduces known-fluid properties within tolerance.\n\n**Restructuring**:\n- sequential_part: \"each candidate fluid's MD trajectory is one timestep after another; verifying 10^3 candidates one at a time chains that 10^3 times\" (18 words max).\n- method: \"none\" — candidates are independent; the plan verifies all candidates side by side, one per GPU, with domain decomposition within each GPU. Actually, I could also mention Green-Kubo needs long trajectories which are sequential, but that's within-candidate, not restructurable.\n\nHmm wait, but there might be a subtlety: the framework's restructuring is about turning sequential into parallel. The honest answer here: the screening is embarrassingly parallel (method = none). But I should still describe the sequential_part (the per-candidate trajectory and the pipeline rounds).\n\nLet me reconsider: is there any sequential dependency? The pipeline is: generate → filter (predictor) → MD verify → (optionally) retrain predictor with MD data → generate more. If I do active learning, there's a sequential loop. But for a first study, I can do a single pass (generate all, filter once, verify all) = fully parallelizable after generation. So method = \"none\" with the note that the plan runs independent candidates side by side.\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether to make it more interesting. The prompt's restructuring instructions strongly suggest I should consider whether there IS a sequential part that could be restructured. The most honest answer:\n\nsequential_part: \"One long MD trajectory per candidate runs timestep after timestep; screening all candidates serially repeats this chain 10^3 times.\"\n\nmethod: \"none\" — because each candidate's simulation is independent, so run all candidates at once, one per GPU (and each GPU's domain-decomposed).\n\nI'll go with method = \"none\". That means:\n- compute_tested = \"yes\" (the test varies number of candidates, which parallel compute buys directly).\n- restructuring.condition, condition_phase, baseline_sequential, baseline_copies: these are only needed \"if method is not none\". Since method = \"none\", I can omit them (the schema only requires sequential_part and method). Good, that simplifies.\n\nWait, let me re-read: \"If method is not none, also give condition, condition_phase, baseline_sequential, baseline_copies, baseline_parallel...\". And compute_axis description says \"If restructuring.method is not none: the chain length or the number of runs the answer needs.\" \n\nHmm, but my compute_axis is \"candidates verified by MD\" even with method=\"none\". The schema for compute_axis just has quantity/unit/levels. The description is guidance. With method=\"none\", the compute axis = \"number of independent candidate MD simulations\" = number of runs. That's consistent (\"the number of runs the answer needs\").\n\nOK so compute_tested = \"yes\". Good.\n\n**predictions**:\n- if_lever (22 words): \"More candidates verified keeps raising the best cooling figure of merit above commercial fluids, as fresh molecules beat them.\" \n- if_not (25 words): \"The best figure of merit plateaus by a few hundred candidates because predictor filtering and force-field error cap how good MD-ranked fluids get.\"\n- decision_rule: \"Rank candidates by MD figure of merit; lever if the best FOM at 10^3 candidates beats the best at 10^2 by more than the MD noise (e.g., >5%), and keeps beating commercial; not lever if 10^3 ≈ 10^2 within noise.\"\n- noise_rule: \"Run 3 independent replicas per candidate; compare the best-FOM across levels only if the improvement exceeds the replica-to-replica standard error of the FOM.\"\n- expected: if_not? or unsure. I think \"unsure\" with expected_why = \"Force fields are good enough to rank viscosities of simple esters/alkanes, but the predictor's hit rate at 10^3+ candidates is untested.\"\n\nActually, let me think about what I expect. The Org-Mol paper shows predictors with R²>0.92 for these properties, and they screened 6M → 461 candidates and validated 2 experimentally with good agreement. So the predictor is quite good. The force field (e.g., GAFF/OPLS) can compute viscosity/thermal conductivity for simple organics with maybe 10-30% accuracy — enough to rank but not to resolve 5% differences. So the \"hit rate against commercial fluids\" is likely capped by force-field accuracy: MD can tell you which candidates are clearly better (low viscosity), but can't distinguish 0.140 vs 0.145 W/m·K thermal conductivity reliably.\n\nSo my expectation: if_not — the hit rate is capped by force-field error, because MD transport properties have ~10-20% uncertainty and commercial fluids are already near the Pareto front of achievable properties for simple molecules. But there's genuine uncertainty because no one has run 10^3 MD-verified candidates.\n\nI'll set expected = \"if_not\" with reason: force-field accuracy (~10-20% on transport) can't resolve the small margins by which candidates beat commercial fluids, so hits plateau.\n\nActually, let me reconsider — the framing of \"if_lever\" vs \"if_not\" is about whether MORE COMPUTE changes the answer. The lever question: does more compute (more candidates verified) buy more/better hits? \n\n- if_lever: more candidates → more hits (the answer keeps improving with compute).\n- if_not: more candidates → hits plateau (predictor/force-field error caps it).\n\nMy expectation is genuinely uncertain but I lean toward \"the hit rate keeps rising but slowly\" — the generative space is huge (millions of esters), and the best candidates found at 10^3 vs 10^2 vs 10^4 could improve as you sample more of the tail. But force-field noise limits resolution.\n\nI'll go with \"unsure\" and be honest: this is exactly the unknown the option flags.\n\nHmm, but the schema says expected must be one of if_lever/if_not/unsure. I'll pick \"unsure\".\n\nActually wait — let me reconsider. Let me re-read: \"predictions.expected is the outcome you expect, if_lever, if_not or unsure, with one sentence why.\"\n\nI'll go \"unsure\": \"No published run has MD-verified 10^3 candidates, so it is unknown whether the hit rate saturates or keeps rising; both are plausible.\"\n\n**scored_against**: \"Measured viscosity, thermal conductivity and dielectric data for known commercial single-phase fluids (e.g., Novec 7000/7500, PAO-2, esters) held out of predictor training.\"\n\n**anchor**: \"MD reproduces a known fluid's viscosity and thermal conductivity within stated tolerance\" — e.g., reproduce Novec 7000 or n-hexadecane or PAO-2 viscosity to within ±20-30%, and the FOM ranking of known commercial fluids. Actually a cleaner anchor: reproduce the experimental viscosity of a known alkane/ester (from NIST) within tolerance, since that's what \"scored against\" measures. \n\nPublished value for anchor: Novec 7000 thermal conductivity 0.075 W/m·K, viscosity 0.32 cSt (from 3M datasheet). But Novec 7000 is a fluorinated ether — hard to parameterize. Better anchor: a simple hydrocarbon/ester with well-known MD results. E.g., n-hexadecane viscosity ~3 cSt, or PAO-2. \n\nActually let me use the Org-Mol paper's validated candidates or a NIST reference. Simpler: anchor on n-decane or n-hexadecane viscosity and thermal conductivity from NIST WebBook or a standard reference. I need a citable number.\n\nLet me search for a citable experimental value for viscosity/thermal conductivity of a simple alkane or ester that MD would reproduce. n-hexadecane: thermal conductivity ~0.14 W/m·K, viscosity ~3.03 cP at 25°C. Or PAO-2: kinematic viscosity 2 cSt at 100°C (from Ravikumar paper: \"PAO-2 with a kinematic viscosity of approximately 2 cSt at 373 K\").\n\nLet me get a solid citable anchor. The Ravikumar nanofluid paper gives \"PAO-2 with a kinematic viscosity of approximately 2 cSt at 373 K\" — but that's at 100°C. \n\nActually, the cleanest anchor for MD: reproduce known literature MD values? No — the anchor should be \"a published number or an independent code the setup reproduces first.\" \n\nI'll anchor on: reproduce the experimentally measured viscosity and thermal conductivity of n-hexadecane (or another simple liquid) to within ±20%, and reproduce the Org-Mol paper's claim that predictors reach R²>0.92 on held-out data (the predictor check). Let me get citable n-hexadecane numbers.\n\nActually, let me use a simpler, well-known anchor with a URL: NIST or a published value. Let me search for \"n-hexadecane viscosity thermal conductivity 25°C value\".\n\nActually, maybe simpler: use the Novec 7000 values already gathered (0.075 W/m·K, 0.32 cSt) as the baseline property values, and the anchor is \"the MD pipeline reproduces the known fluid's viscosity and thermal conductivity within ±25%\" using a force field suitable for the fluid class. But Novec 7000 is fluorinated ether — MD force field might be less reliable.\n\nHmm. Given the plan focuses on hydrocarbons and esters (PFAS-free), the anchor should be a hydrocarbon/ester. Let me use PAO-2 or a simple ester. Let me search quickly for a citable value.\n\nActually, I realize I should be time-efficient. I have enough to write a strong plan. Let me do a couple more targeted fetches/searches:\n1. Fetch the npj Org-Mol paper for exact quotes (I have good quotes from search already; maybe fetch to confirm exact numbers 6M, 8569, 461).\n2. Get a citable n-alkane viscosity/thermal conductivity value for the anchor (NIST or a paper).\n3. Try the eprint 31955 once more.\n\nLet me also decide the exact GROMACS/OpenMM and force field: OpenMM or GROMACS with GAFF2 or OPLS-AA or the TraPPE force field (TraPPE-UA is standard for alkanes/esters transport properties). Actually TraPPE-UA is THE standard force field for hydrocarbon transport properties, and its accuracy for viscosity/thermal conductivity is well documented (within ~10-20%). Let me cite something for TraPPE-UA viscosity accuracy. That would support the \"force-field error caps\" hypothesis.\n\nLet me search for \"TraPPE united atom viscosity accuracy alkane percent\" or \"Green-Kubo thermal conductivity alkane force field accuracy\".\n\nLet me also reconsider the FOM. The Mouromtseff number for single-phase forced convection. I found the Lee et al. 1988 IEEE paper \"Fluid selection and property effects in single and two-phase immersion cooling\" which derives FOMs. Let me get the exact FOM form. Search gave the abstract but not the formula. I can define the FOM myself as DERIVED using the Mouromtseff form. But to CITE it, I need the formula from a page.\n\nActually, I can define the FOM cleanly as a DERIVED expression over the measured properties, using the standard Mouromtseff number for turbulent forced convection: FOM = ρ^0.8 · k^0.67 · cp^0.33 / μ^0.47. I should cite a source for this form. The Lee 1988 paper is the canonical source but I don't have the exact formula from a fetched page. Let me search for \"Mouromtseff number formula forced convection\" to get a citable page with the formula.\n\nLet me do these searches now in parallel.\n\nActually, I want to be efficient. Let me do a batch of searches/fetches:\n1. Fetch npj paper (nature.com) for exact numbers.\n2. Search Mouromtseff number formula.\n3. Search TraPPE force field viscosity/thermal conductivity accuracy for alkanes.\n4. Search n-hexadecane or n-decane viscosity thermal conductivity experimental value."
